<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Alfonsina Strada was removed from the classification of the 1924 Giro d’Italia, yet continued all the way to Milan."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/stories/alfonsina-strada-1924/alfonsina-strada-rulli.jpg"/><title>The woman the Giro could not get rid of · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/alfonsina-strada-giro-1924/"/><main><article class="skibby-page">
<header class="skibby-hero"><p class="section-label">Giro d’Italia · 1924</p><h1>The woman the Giro<br/>could not <em>get rid of</em></h1><p>Alfonsina Strada disappeared from the results in Perugia. Alfonsina herself did not.</p></header><figure class="skibby-image"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/alfonsina-strada-1924/alfonsina-strada-rulli.jpg`} alt="Alfonsina Strada training on rollers around 1924"/><figcaption>Alfonsina Strada on rollers, around 1924. Unknown photographer · public domain via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="skibby-opening"><p class="section-label">Perugia, long after dark</p><p>She had arrived too late. The rules said her Giro was over. The next morning, however, Alfonsina Strada was back with her bicycle and continued towards Milan.</p></section><section class="skibby-story"><aside><strong>1924</strong><span>One woman<br/>among 89 men</span></aside><div><h2>A Giro without the biggest names</h2><p>The 1924 Giro d’Italia began with a dispute. Bicycle manufacturers and the organising newspaper <em>La Gazzetta dello Sport</em> had fallen out, and the professional teams stayed away. The race instead opened to the so-called <em>isolati</em> — riders who had to manage much of the journey without a large team around them.</p><p>Among the 90 starters was Alfonsina Strada, number 72. She was 33, experienced in racing against men and had previously ridden the Giro di Lombardia. No woman had started the Giro before. None has done so since in the men’s event.</p><h2>The name was no disguise</h2><p>The story is often told as though the organisers only discovered her sex after the start list had been printed as “Alfonsin Strada”. It makes for a neat surprise, but is not the whole explanation. The organisers knew her, and her presence brought attention to a race without its stars.</p><p>The road was no easier for that. She rode through rain, cold and roads capable of breaking both bicycle and rider. On the stage from L’Aquila to Perugia, she fell several times and damaged the handlebar.</p><blockquote>The later, famous repair with a piece of broom handle also appears in the Giro’s own history. The more important improvisation was that she continued after the results had given up on her.</blockquote><h2>Outside the time limit</h2><p>The stage to Perugia was 296 kilometres. Alfonsina arrived late, bruised and outside the time limit. She could no longer count as an official competitor.</p><p>Race director Emilio Colombo allowed her to continue outside the competition and, according to the Giro’s account, paid for her accommodation. The decision was both humane and practical: spectators came to see her, and the newspaper had found a story larger than the placings.</p></div></section>
<section class="skibby-facts">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section><section class="skibby-context"><div><p class="section-label">A rider without a placing</p><h2>She completed the route — but not in the classification</h2></div><div><p>The wording matters. Alfonsina was removed from the official competition in Perugia. She nevertheless continued along the same route as the peloton and reached Milan. The Giro’s own archive describes this double status: outside the classification, but with the entire road behind her.</p><p>Ninety riders started. Only 30 reached Milan when Alfonsina is included. Giuseppe Enrici won. The results remember him. The story also remembers the rider who could no longer receive a placing.</p></div></section>
<section class="skibby-return"><p class="section-label">More than a curiosity</p><blockquote>She was not invited into an equal race. She found an opening in a sport built almost entirely by and for men.</blockquote><div><p>It is easy to turn Alfonsina into a pleasant tale about a broom handle and a stubborn cyclist. Her participation showed something larger. Women could endure the same roads and distances even when the framework was not made for them.</p><p>That she never had a successor in the men’s Giro says as much about cycling’s institutions as it does about her exceptional ride.</p></div></section><section class="skibby-finish"><div><p class="section-label">Milan</p><h2>Larger than the results</h2></div><div><p>When Alfonsina reached the finish, she was not an official finisher. Even so, she was received as one of the race’s central figures. Spectators had followed her struggle, and the newspapers had discovered that the back of the peloton could contain a story the winner could not take away.</p><p>A century later, the paradox remains: the Giro removed her name from the classification, but could not remove her from its own history.</p><p><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1920-1930/`}>Read about cycling in the 1920s.</a></p></div></section><nav class="era-bottom-nav" aria-label="Further navigation"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/`}>← All stories</a><a href={`${base}/en/stories/campagnolo-quick-release/`}>Next story →</a></nav></article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
